<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minaret of Jam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1190s</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minaret of Jam is a brick minaret about 65 metres high in a remote valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, beside the Hari Rud. Built by the Ghurid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a>, probably in the 1190s, it is covered with geometric brickwork and the Qur'anic chapter of Maryam. It probably marks the site of the Ghurid capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozkoh">Firozkoh</a>, and in 2002 it became Afghanistan's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minaret-of-jam.jpg" alt="Minaret of Jam" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: david adamec · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jam_afghan_architecture_harirud_brick.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ghurids controlled what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in the 12th and 13th centuries, together with parts of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. The Minaret of Jam probably stands at the site of their capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozkoh">Firozkoh</a>. Its Arabic dating inscription is unclear, but a Kufic inscription on the north face gives the year five hundred ninety, equivalent to 27 December 1193 to 16 December 1194. The British archaeologist Ralph Pinder-Wilson believed it commemorated the victory of Mu'izz ad-Din, Ghiyath ud-Din's brother, over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a>, which opened the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>The minaret belongs to a group of about 60 minarets and towers built between the 11th and 13th centuries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Some are thought to have been symbols of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>'s victory. Its architect, Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Naysaburi, is named in an inscription.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The circular tower rests on an octagonal base. It had two wooden balconies and was topped by a lantern. It is built of baked brick, stucco, terracotta and turquoise glazed tiles, and its outer face is decorated with floral and geometric designs, with bands of Kufic and Naskhi calligraphy and intersecting rhomboidal knots. Inside, a central pillar carries double spiral staircases of 159 steps each, leading up to the balconies, and above them six vaulted brick platforms.</p><p>The inscriptions include the Muslim confession of faith in the uppermost band, a verse from the surah al-Saff promising help from God and present victory, the names and titles of the Ghurid sultan Ghiyath ad-Din Muhammad bin Sam, and interlaced bands of the surah Maryam. Its form resembles the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> minarets built by Masud III, and it is thought to have inspired the Qutub Minar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The minaret was assumed to be attached to the Friday Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozkoh">Firozkoh</a>, which the chronicler Minhaj-i Siraj Juzjani says was washed away in a flash flood before the Mongol sieges of the early 13th century. Juzjani says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed Firuzkuh in 1222. Sir Thomas Holdich reported the minaret in 1886 while working for the Afghan Boundary Commission, but it did not come to wide attention until 1957, through the work of the French archaeologists André Maricq and Gaston Wiet. The landscape around it holds the ruins of a palace, fortifications, a pottery kiln and a Jewish cemetery. The site was nominated in 2002 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>'s first World Heritage site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minaret of Jam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Minaret of Jam</b>, about 65 metres high and decorated with brick patterns and the Qur'anic chapter of Maryam, was raised in the remote valley that probably held Firozkoh, most likely in the 1190s, and is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
